## 8. BIOS/UEFI Preparation

BIOS/UEFI firmware controls important hardware settings before the operating system starts.

Before installation, inspect the firmware configuration.

### Settings to Review

| Setting | Purpose | Planned configuration |
|---|---|---|
| Boot mode | Controls boot method | UEFI where supported |
| Boot priority | Determines boot device order | USB during installation |
| CPU virtualization | Enables hardware-assisted virtualization | Enable if required |
| IOMMU | Supports certain device passthrough configurations | Optional |
| Secure Boot | Helps verify the boot software | Follow platform requirements |
| SATA mode | Controls SATA controller operation | Verify compatibility |
| Wake-on-LAN | Allows network-based wake-up | Optional |

### Firmware Procedure

1. Restart the physical server.
2. Open the BIOS/UEFI setup utility.
3. Record the existing firmware version.
4. Identify the boot configuration.
5. Check CPU virtualization settings.
6. Check IOMMU settings if needed.
7. Review storage controller settings.
8. Save only the changes that are required.
9. Restart the system.

The exact menu names differ between motherboard manufacturers.

Do not change firmware settings randomly. Consult the motherboard manual.

