## 12. Initial Proxmox VE Configuration

This section applies only if Proxmox VE is selected and installed on the physical server.

### 12.1 Access the Management Interface

From another computer on the local network:

1. Open a web browser.
2. Enter the Proxmox management address shown during installation.
3. Use HTTPS.
4. Review the browser certificate warning in the context of the locally managed server.
5. Sign in with the administrative account established during installation.
6. Verify that the physical node appears in the management interface.

Do not expose the management interface directly to the public internet.

### 12.2 Review the Node

Check:

- CPU model and core count.
- Memory capacity and usage.
- Storage availability.
- Network interfaces.
- System status.
- Hardware and system warnings.

### 12.3 Review Storage

Understand the storage definitions displayed by the platform.

Possible storage uses include:

- ISO images.
- Virtual machine disks.
- Container templates.
- Backups.
- Application data.

Do not assume that storage configured for virtual machine disks is automatically a separate backup destination.

### 12.4 Review Networking

Understand the difference between:

- Physical network interfaces.
- Linux bridges.
- Virtual machine network interfaces.
- VLAN-aware networking.
- Management network connectivity.

A Linux bridge allows virtual machines to communicate through a network interface according to the bridge configuration.

Do not change the management network configuration remotely without a recovery plan. An incorrect change can make the management interface unreachable.

### 12.5 Create the First Test Virtual Machine

Only after the base installation and network configuration are verified:

1. Download an operating system ISO through the supported management process.
2. Select the server node.
3. Open the virtual machine creation workflow.
4. Assign a unique VM identifier.
5. Give the VM a descriptive name.
6. Select the installation ISO.
7. Allocate an appropriate number of CPU cores.
8. Allocate RAM while leaving sufficient memory for the physical host.
9. Select an appropriate virtual disk.
10. Configure a virtual network interface.
11. Review the settings.
12. Create the VM.
13. Start it.
14. Complete its operating system installation.
15. Confirm that it can access the network.

Start with one small test virtual machine rather than creating several at once.

