## 13. Initial Security Configuration

A home lab should be treated as real infrastructure, even when it is used primarily for learning.

### 13.1 Account Security

- [ ] Use strong, unique passwords.
- [ ] Avoid shared administrator accounts.
- [ ] Use normal accounts for routine activities.
- [ ] Restrict administrative privileges.
- [ ] Use SSH keys where appropriate.
- [ ] Protect recovery information.

### 13.2 Network Security

- [ ] Keep management interfaces on a trusted network.
- [ ] Use firewall rules.
- [ ] Do not expose management ports directly to the internet.
- [ ] Restrict access to services that do not need public access.
- [ ] Understand the router's port-forwarding configuration.
- [ ] Consider a separate management network as the lab grows.

### 13.3 Software Security

- [ ] Install security updates.
- [ ] Remove unnecessary services.
- [ ] Use supported software releases.
- [ ] Review running services.
- [ ] Monitor system logs.
- [ ] Document configuration changes.
- [ ] Back up important configuration files.

### 13.4 Public Internet Access

Do not make the management interface publicly accessible merely to enable remote administration.

Safer remote-access designs may use:

- A properly configured VPN.
- A trusted private network.
- Strong authentication.
- Firewall restrictions.
- A carefully maintained remote-access service.

Remote access should be planned separately from basic installation.

