## 14. Backup and Recovery Planning

A server installation is not complete simply because it boots successfully.

Plan how the system will be recovered after accidental deletion, disk failure, corruption or a failed update.

### 14.1 What Should Be Backed Up?

Depending on the platform, consider backing up:

- Virtual machines.
- Container data.
- Databases.
- Application data.
- Network configuration.
- System configuration.
- Important scripts.
- Documentation.
- Encryption keys and recovery information, stored securely.

### 14.2 Backup Destination

Prefer a backup destination that is independent of the primary server storage.

Possible destinations include:

- External storage.
- A separate backup server.
- Network-attached storage.
- A trusted remote backup service.

A second partition on the same physical drive is not an independent backup.

### 14.3 Backup Verification

- [ ] Configure a backup schedule.
- [ ] Identify what is included.
- [ ] Identify what is excluded.
- [ ] Protect backup access.
- [ ] Record backup results.
- [ ] Test restoring a file.
- [ ] Test restoring a virtual machine where applicable.
- [ ] Review backup retention.

A backup that has never been tested may not be recoverable when needed.

